/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/linux-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("linux-plus", [["lx1",0,1,"A new USB network adapter is not working, and the admin wants to confirm whether its driver module is currently loaded in the running kernel. Which command shows this?",["modinfo","depmod","lsmod","insmod"],2,"lsmod lists the modules loaded right now (it reads /proc/modules). modinfo only describes a module file on disk, whether or not it is loaded.","1.0 System management: kernel modules"],["lx2",0,1,"On a UEFI server, which component loads the Linux kernel and the initramfs into memory during boot?",["systemd running as PID 1","The dracut utility","The /etc/fstab file","The GRUB2 boot loader"],3,"Firmware starts the boot loader (GRUB2 or shim + GRUB2), which loads the kernel and initramfs. systemd starts only after the kernel is running; dracut builds initramfs images but does not boot them.","1.0 System management: boot process"],["lx3",0,1,"After adding a storage driver that must be available at boot on a RHEL-family server, the admin needs to rebuild the initial RAM disk. Which command does this?",["grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg","depmod -a","dracut -f","mkfs.ext4 /boot"],2,"dracut -f regenerates the initramfs for the running kernel. grub2-mkconfig rebuilds the boot menu, not the initramfs, so the new driver would still be missing early in boot.","1.0 System management: boot process"],["lx4",0,1,"According to the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, where should an application write its variable log files?",["/usr/log","/var/log","/etc/log","/opt/log"],1,"/var holds variable data such as logs, spools and caches. /etc is for configuration and /usr is meant to be read-only shared program data.","1.0 System management: filesystem hierarchy"],["lx5",0,1,"An admin wants to mount a new disk in /etc/fstab by UUID so the entry survives device name changes. Which command shows the partition's UUID?",["df -h /dev/sdc1","mount -a /dev/sdc1","blkid /dev/sdc1","fdisk -n /dev/sdc1"],2,"blkid prints the UUID, label and filesystem type of a block device (lsblk -f works too). df shows usage of mounted filesystems only and does not print UUIDs.","1.0 System management: storage"],["lx6",0,1,"The /data logical volume in volume group vg_data is almost full. A new disk, /dev/sdc, has been attached. Which sequence adds the space to /data with the least disruption?",["vgcreate vg_new /dev/sdc; lvcreate; mkfs the new LV","fdisk to merge /dev/sdc into the LV, then run resize2fs","pvcreate /dev/sdc; vgextend vg_data /dev/sdc; lvextend -r","mdadm --create with /dev/sdc, then mount it over /data"],2,"Initialize the disk as a physical volume, add it to the existing volume group, then grow the LV; -r resizes the filesystem in the same step. Creating a new VG gives a separate volume instead of growing /data.","1.0 System management: LVM"],["lx7",0,1,"An XFS filesystem on a logical volume must be made 20 GB smaller. What is the supported approach?",["Run xfs_growfs -D with a smaller block count","Unmount it and run resize2fs with the new size","Back it up, recreate it smaller, and restore","Run lvreduce -r on it while it stays mounted"],2,"XFS can grow but cannot shrink. The only way is back up, recreate a smaller filesystem and restore. resize2fs works on ext2/3/4, not XFS.","1.0 System management: filesystems"],["lx8",0,1,"Running ls -li in a directory shows: \"1311 -rw-r--r-- 2 app app 812 data.txt\" and \"1311 -rw-r--r-- 2 app app 812 report.txt\". What is the relationship between the two files?",["report.txt is a symbolic link to data.txt","They are hard links to the same inode","They are copies made with cp -p","They live on two different filesystems"],1,"Both names share inode 1311 and the link count is 2, which is what a hard link looks like. A symbolic link would have its own inode, an l file type and an arrow to its target.","1.0 System management: files and links"],["lx9",0,1,"Which command creates a gzip-compressed archive of /etc that keeps file permissions?",["tar -czpf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -xzpf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -tzvf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -cjpf etc.tar.gz /etc"],0,"-c creates, -z uses gzip, -p keeps permissions and -f names the archive. -x would extract and -j would compress with bzip2 despite the .gz name.","1.0 System management: backup and restore"],["lx10",0,1,"A web root must be mirrored nightly to host backup so files deleted from the source are also removed from the copy. Which command does this?",["scp -r /srv/web backup:/srv/web","cp -au /srv/web backup:/srv/web","rsync -av --delete /srv/web/ backup:/srv/web/","rsync -av --remove-source-files /srv/web/ backup:/srv/web/"],2,"rsync --delete removes files from the destination that no longer exist in the source, giving a true mirror. --remove-source-files deletes the source files after copying, which destroys the original.","1.0 System management: backup and restore"],["lx11",0,1,"Before replacing a failing disk, an admin wants a block-level image of all of /dev/sdb saved to /backup. Which command does this?",["dd if=/backup/sdb.img of=/dev/sdb bs=4M","tar -cf /backup/sdb.img /dev/sdb","cp --sparse=always /dev/sdb1 /backup/","dd if=/dev/sdb of=/backup/sdb.img bs=4M"],3,"dd reads the input device (if=) and writes it to the output file (of=). Swapping if and of would overwrite the disk with the image, the opposite of what is needed.","1.0 System management: backup and restore"],["lx12",0,1,"On a server managed by NetworkManager, which command sets a static IPv4 address that survives a reboot?",["ip addr add 10.0.0.5/24 dev eth0 && ip link set dev eth0 up","nmcli con mod eth0 ipv4.addresses 10.0.0.5/24 ipv4.method manual","ifconfig eth0 10.0.0.5 netmask 255.255.255.0","echo '10.0.0.5 eth0' >> /etc/hosts"],1,"nmcli connection modify saves the setting in the connection profile, so it persists. ip addr add changes only the running kernel state and is lost at reboot or when the connection restarts.","1.0 System management: network configuration"],["lx13",0,1,"An admin needs to rename a server to web01 permanently. Which command should be used?",["hostname web01","hostnamectl set-hostname web01","echo web01 > /proc/sys/kernel/hostname","echo web01 >> /etc/hosts"],1,"hostnamectl set-hostname writes /etc/hostname and updates the running name. The hostname command and /proc change only the running value until the next reboot.","1.0 System management: network configuration"],["lx14",0,1,"A server keeps resolving app.local through DNS even though /etc/hosts has a different entry for it. Which file controls the order in which these sources are checked?",["/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/nsswitch.conf","/etc/hosts.allow","/etc/sysconfig/network"],1,"The hosts: line in /etc/nsswitch.conf sets the lookup order (for example files dns). resolv.conf lists DNS servers and search domains but does not decide whether /etc/hosts is read first.","1.0 System management: network configuration"],["lx15",0,1,"Which command sends both standard output and standard error of cmd to log.txt?",["cmd > log.txt 2>&1","cmd 2>&1 > log.txt","cmd | tee log.txt","cmd 2> log.txt"],0,"Redirections are processed left to right: stdout goes to the file, then stderr is pointed at the same place. With 2>&1 first, stderr is copied to the terminal before stdout moves, so errors still reach the screen.","1.0 System management: shell operations"],["lx16",0,1,"What does cut -d: -f1,7 print for the line \"root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash\"?",["root /bin/bash","root:x:0:0","/root:/bin/bash","root:/bin/bash"],3,"cut splits on the colon delimiter and prints fields 1 and 7, joined with the same delimiter. Field 6 is /root, the home directory, not field 7.","1.0 System management: shell operations"],["lx17",0,1,"A shell variable APP_ENV=prod is set at the prompt, but a script started from that shell sees it as empty. What fixes this?",["set APP_ENV=prod","export APP_ENV=prod","local APP_ENV=prod","alias APP_ENV=prod"],1,"export marks the variable for the environment of child processes. Without it, the variable is shell-local; local only works inside functions and alias defines a command shortcut.","1.0 System management: shell operations"],["lx18",0,1,"On a KVM host, which command lists every defined virtual machine, including ones that are shut off?",["qemu-img info --all","virsh list --all","virt-install --list","lsmod | grep kvm"],1,"virsh list shows running domains; --all adds inactive ones. lsmod | grep kvm only confirms the KVM modules are loaded.","1.0 System management: virtualization"],["lx19",0,1,"A VM disk image should grow only as data is written and should support internal snapshots. Which image format fits?",["raw","ISO 9660","tar.gz","qcow2"],3,"qcow2 (QEMU copy-on-write) is thin-provisioned and supports snapshots and backing files. Raw images are simple and fast but have no built-in snapshot support.","1.0 System management: virtualization"],["lx20",0,1,"Which command finds files under /var larger than 100 MB that were modified in the last 7 days?",["find /var -size -100M -mtime +7","find /var -size +100M -mtime -7","find /var -size 100M -atime 7","locate /var -size +100M -mtime -7"],1,"+100M means larger than 100 MiB and -mtime -7 means modified less than 7 days ago. Reversing the signs finds small, old files; locate searches a name database and has no size test.","1.0 System management: files and directories"],["lx21",0,1,"A USB backup disk listed in /etc/fstab is sometimes unplugged, and the server then drops to emergency mode at boot. Which mount option prevents this?",["noexec","defaults","nofail","sync"],2,"nofail tells systemd not to fail the boot if the device is missing. noexec only blocks running programs from the filesystem.","1.0 System management: storage"],["lx22",0,2,"A new developer needs an account with a home directory and bash as the login shell. Which command creates it?",["usermod -m -s /bin/bash dev1","useradd -M -s /bin/bash dev1","useradd -m -s /bin/bash dev1","groupadd -m -s /bin/bash dev1"],2,"useradd -m creates the home directory and -s sets the shell. -M explicitly skips the home directory, and usermod changes an account that already exists.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts"],["lx23",0,2,"User alice is in several supplementary groups and must also be added to docker without losing the others. Which command is correct?",["usermod -G docker alice","usermod -aG docker alice","groupadd docker alice","chgrp docker alice"],1,"-a (append) with -G adds the group and keeps existing ones. -G alone replaces her whole supplementary group list with just docker.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts"],["lx24",0,2,"Policy requires that alice change her password at least every 90 days. Which command enforces this?",["chage -E 90 alice","chage -M 90 alice","passwd -l alice","usermod -e 90 alice"],1,"chage -M sets the maximum password age in days. -E (and usermod -e) set an account expiry date, which disables the account rather than forcing a password change.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts"],["lx25",0,2,"The /etc/shadow entry for alice begins \"alice:!$6$Qw...\". What does the ! before the hash mean?",["The password has already expired","The account password is locked","The account has no password set","The user must change it at next login"],1,"passwd -l or usermod -L adds ! in front of the hash so no password can match. An expired password is shown by the aging fields, not by a ! prefix.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts"],["lx26",0,2,"Nginx has just been installed. It must start now and also start at every boot. Which single command does both?",["systemctl start nginx","systemctl unmask nginx","systemctl enable --now nginx","systemctl reload-or-restart nginx"],2,"enable --now creates the boot-time links and starts the unit immediately. start alone runs it now but it will not come back after a reboot.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd"],["lx27",0,2,"systemctl status shows: \"Loaded: loaded (/usr/lib/systemd/system/cups.service; disabled; preset: enabled)\" and \"Active: active (running)\". What is true?",["It starts at boot but is stopped right now","It is running now but will not start at boot","It is masked and cannot be started at all","It failed and systemd is restarting it"],1,"disabled describes boot-time behavior and active (running) the current state. The preset only says what the distribution's default would be.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd"],["lx28",0,2,"An admin must change a setting in a vendor-supplied systemd unit so the change survives package updates. What is the best method?",["Run systemctl edit to create a drop-in override","Edit the unit file in /usr/lib/systemd/system","Add the setting to the /etc/rc.local script","Copy the unit into /run/systemd and edit it"],0,"systemctl edit writes a drop-in under /etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/ and reloads systemd. Files in /usr/lib/systemd/system are replaced by package updates, and /run is cleared at reboot.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd"],["lx29",0,2,"In a systemd .timer unit, which directive schedules the job to run once a day at midnight?",["OnBootSec=daily","OnCalendar=daily","ExecStart=daily","WantedBy=daily"],1,"OnCalendar sets wall-clock schedules, and daily means 00:00 every day. OnBootSec takes a time span after boot, not a calendar expression.","2.0 Services and user management: scheduling"],["lx30",0,2,"A crontab contains \"30 2 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/backup.sh\". When does the script run?",["At 01:30 every second day","At 02:30 on the 1st of every month","Every 30 minutes on Mondays after 2 a.m.","At 02:30 every Monday"],3,"The fields are minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, so this is minute 30, hour 2, any date, any month, weekday 1 (Monday). The 1st of the month would go in the third field.","2.0 Services and user management: scheduling"],["lx31",0,2,"A runaway process must be asked to shut down cleanly so it can flush its buffers. Which signal should be sent first?",["SIGKILL (9)","SIGSTOP (19)","SIGCONT (18)","SIGTERM (15)"],3,"SIGTERM asks the process to exit and can be handled for cleanup; it is the default for kill. SIGKILL cannot be caught, so the process gets no chance to clean up. Use it only if SIGTERM fails.","2.0 Services and user management: processes"],["lx32",0,2,"A batch job with PID 4321 is slowing interactive users. Which command lowers its CPU priority without stopping it?",["renice -n -10 -p 4321","nice -n 10 4321","kill -STOP 4321","renice -n 10 -p 4321"],3,"A higher nice value means lower priority, so renice to 10 makes the job yield CPU. A negative value raises priority, and nice only starts new commands.","2.0 Services and user management: processes"],["lx33",0,2,"A long-running command was started in the foreground. How can it be moved to the background without restarting it?",["Press Ctrl+C, then run bg","Press Ctrl+Z, then run bg","Press Ctrl+D, then run fg","Run nohup %1 from the prompt"],1,"Ctrl+Z suspends the job and bg resumes it in the background. Ctrl+C sends SIGINT and usually ends the job, so there is nothing left to resume.","2.0 Services and user management: processes"],["lx34",0,2,"On a RHEL-family server, the dig command is missing. Which command shows which package provides it?",["rpm -qa dig","dnf info dig","rpm -ql dig","dnf provides '*/bin/dig'"],3,"dnf provides searches repository metadata for the package that ships a file (bind-utils here). rpm -qa and rpm -ql query only packages that are already installed.","2.0 Services and user management: software management"],["lx35",0,2,"After adding a new APT repository on Ubuntu, which command should run before installing a package from it?",["apt upgrade","apt autoremove","apt update","dpkg --configure -a"],2,"apt update downloads the package lists, including the new repository's. Without it, APT does not know the new packages exist. apt upgrade installs newer versions of packages already on the system.","2.0 Services and user management: software management"],["lx36",0,2,"An admin finds an unfamiliar /etc/ssh/sshd_config change and wants to know which installed RPM owns that file. Which command answers this?",["rpm -ql /etc/ssh/sshd_config","rpm -qi /etc/ssh/sshd_config","dnf search sshd_config","rpm -qf /etc/ssh/sshd_config"],3,"rpm -qf (query file) returns the owning package. rpm -ql lists the files in a package, so it expects a package name, not a file path.","2.0 Services and user management: software management"],["lx37",0,2,"A container runs nginx on port 80 inside the container. Users must reach it on host port 8080. Which podman run option does this?",["-p 80:8080","--expose 8080","--network host:8080","-p 8080:80"],3,"The format is -p hostPort:containerPort. -p 80:8080 would publish host port 80 to a container port that nothing listens on.","2.0 Services and user management: containers"],["lx38",0,2,"A database container loses its data whenever it is recreated from a new image version. What is the correct fix?",["Commit the running container to a new image every night","Keep the data in the container's writable layer","Raise the container's memory and CPU limits","Mount a volume for the data directory"],3,"Named volumes or bind mounts live outside the container's lifecycle, so the data survives when the container is replaced. The writable layer is deleted with the container.","2.0 Services and user management: containers"],["lx39",0,2,"Which command shows log messages for the sshd unit from the current boot only?",["journalctl -u sshd -b","journalctl -k -u sshd","dmesg --unit sshd","systemctl list-units sshd"],0,"-u filters by unit and -b limits output to the current boot. -k shows kernel messages only, which would hide sshd's own entries.","2.0 Services and user management: logging"],["lx40",0,3,"After running chmod 750 deploy.sh, what permission string does ls -l show for the file?",["-rwxr-xr-x","-rwxr-x---","-rw-r-x---","-rwx-w----"],1,"7 = rwx for the owner, 5 = r-x for the group, 0 = --- for others. -rwxr-xr-x would be 755.","3.0 Security: file permissions"],["lx41",0,3,"A user's umask is 027. What permissions does a new regular file created with touch receive?",["-rw-r--r--","-rwxr-x---","-rw-rw----","-rw-r-----"],3,"Regular files start at 666 and the umask removes 027, leaving 640. Directories start at 777 and would get 750; that is where the x bits come from.","3.0 Security: file permissions"],["lx42",0,3,"ls -l /usr/bin/passwd shows -rwsr-xr-x root root. What does the s in the owner execute position mean?",["Only the owner can delete or rename it","It runs with the file owner's privileges","It is shared by every group on the system","It stays cached in memory after it exits"],1,"That is the SUID bit: the program runs with the owner's UID (root here), which lets passwd update /etc/shadow. Protection from deletion by other users is what the sticky bit does on directories.","3.0 Security: special permissions"],["lx43",0,3,"Users share /srv/shared, and everyone must be able to create files but must not delete other users' files. Which command sets this up?",["chmod 1777 /srv/shared","chmod 2777 /srv/shared","chmod 4777 /srv/shared","chmod 0777 /srv/shared"],0,"The leading 1 sets the sticky bit, so only a file's owner (or root) can delete or rename it, as on /tmp. 2 is setgid, which controls group inheritance, not deletion.","3.0 Security: special permissions"],["lx44",0,3,"Bob needs read access to report.pdf without changing its owner, group or other permissions. Which command grants it?",["setfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf","getfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf","chmod o+r report.pdf","chown bob report.pdf"],0,"setfacl -m adds an ACL entry for one user only. chmod o+r would let every user read the file, and getfacl only displays ACLs.","3.0 Security: access control lists"],["lx45",0,3,"On an SELinux-enforcing server, files moved from a home directory into /var/www/html return 403 errors, although the permissions look fine. What is the correct fix?",["setenforce 0","restorecon -Rv /var/www/html","chmod -R 777 /var/www/html","semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 80"],1,"mv keeps the original user_home_t label; restorecon resets the files to the default httpd_sys_content_t context. setenforce 0 only hides the problem by turning off enforcement.","3.0 Security: SELinux"],["lx46",0,3,"Which command shows whether SELinux is currently Enforcing, Permissive or Disabled?",["aa-status","setsebool","ausearch","getenforce"],3,"getenforce prints the current mode (sestatus gives more detail). aa-status is the AppArmor equivalent and does not report SELinux.","3.0 Security: SELinux"],["lx47",0,3,"On Ubuntu, an admin wants an AppArmor profile to log violations without blocking them while it is being tuned. Which command does this?",["aa-enforce","aa-disable","setenforce 0","aa-complain"],3,"aa-complain puts the profile in complain mode: violations are logged but allowed. aa-disable unloads the profile completely, so nothing is logged.","3.0 Security: AppArmor"],["lx48",0,3,"An admin ran firewall-cmd --add-service=https and HTTPS worked, but the rule disappeared after a reboot. What went wrong?",["firewalld does not support the https service","The rule was added without --permanent","The zone was set to trusted during boot","SELinux removed the rule at startup"],1,"Without --permanent, firewall-cmd changes only the runtime configuration. Add it with --permanent and then --reload (or use --runtime-to-permanent).","3.0 Security: firewalls"],["lx49",0,3,"An admin connected over SSH is about to turn on ufw for the first time. What must be done first to avoid being locked out?",["Run ufw allow OpenSSH","Run ufw default allow incoming","Run ufw reset, then ufw disable","Run ufw deny 22/tcp for brute force"],0,"ufw's default policy denies incoming traffic, so SSH must be allowed before ufw enable. Allowing all incoming traffic by default defeats the purpose of the firewall.","3.0 Security: firewalls"],["lx50",0,3,"Which sshd_config setting stops the root account from logging in directly over SSH?",["PasswordAuthentication yes","PermitRootLogin no","PermitEmptyPasswords no","UsePAM no"],1,"PermitRootLogin no blocks root logins, so admins sign in as themselves and use sudo. PermitEmptyPasswords no is good practice but does not stop root logging in with a password or key.","3.0 Security: SSH hardening"],["lx51",0,3,"An admin generated an Ed25519 key pair and wants to use key-based login to server01. Which command installs the public key on the server?",["ssh-keygen -R server01","ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","scp ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 admin@server01:","ssh-copy-id admin@server01"],3,"ssh-copy-id appends the public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys with the right permissions. Copying id_ed25519 would expose the private key, which should never leave the client.","3.0 Security: SSH hardening"],["lx52",0,3,"The ops group must be allowed to restart nginx with sudo, and nothing else. What is the safest way to add the rule?",["Append the rule to /etc/sudoers with echo","Add every member of ops to the wheel group","Create /etc/sudoers.d/ops with visudo -f","Edit /etc/sudoers directly in nano as root"],2,"visudo checks syntax before saving, so an error cannot break sudo, and a drop-in keeps the rule separate. Adding users to wheel grants full root, far more than needed.","3.0 Security: privilege escalation"],["lx53",0,3,"On RHEL 9, security policy requires locking an account after five failed login attempts. Which PAM module provides this?",["pam_faillock","pam_limits","pam_pwquality","pam_env"],0,"pam_faillock counts failed attempts and locks the account (settings in /etc/security/faillock.conf). pam_pwquality enforces password complexity, not lockout.","3.0 Security: authentication"],["lx54",0,3,"An admin downloaded an ISO and a SHA256SUMS file from the vendor. Which command confirms the ISO matches the published checksum?",["gpg --symmetric SHA256SUMS","openssl rand -hex 32","sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS","md5sum --encrypt SHA256SUMS"],2,"sha256sum -c recomputes each hash and reports OK or FAILED. Verifying the GPG signature on SHA256SUMS (gpg --verify) proves who published it; --symmetric encrypts a file instead.","3.0 Security: cryptography"],["lx55",0,3,"A laptop's data partition /dev/sdb1 must be encrypted at rest with a passphrase that is asked for at boot. Which command starts this setup?",["cryptsetup luksFormat /dev/sdb1","gpg --encrypt /dev/sdb1","chattr +i /dev/sdb1","mdadm --encrypt /dev/sdb1"],0,"cryptsetup luksFormat creates a LUKS header on the partition. Then luksOpen, mkfs and an /etc/crypttab entry finish the setup. chattr +i makes a file immutable but does not encrypt anything.","3.0 Security: cryptography"],["lx56",0,4,"Which statement best describes idempotence in an Ansible playbook?",["Every task runs on every host in parallel","Tasks run once and are then removed from the playbook","Each run rebuilds the host from a clean image","A second run changes nothing if the host already matches"],3,"Idempotent modules check the current state and act only when it differs, so reruns report ok instead of changed. Parallel execution across hosts is a separate feature (forks).","4.0 Automation: configuration management"],["lx57",0,4,"Which command checks that Ansible can reach and log in to every host in the web inventory group?",["ansible web -m ping","ansible-playbook web -m ping","ansible-galaxy ping web","ansible-vault ping web"],0,"The ansible command runs an ad hoc module against a pattern; the ping module confirms SSH access and a usable Python. ansible-playbook expects a playbook file, not -m.","4.0 Automation: configuration management"],["lx58",0,4,"A playbook needs a database password, and the file will be committed to Git. Which tool should protect the secret?",["ansible-vault","ansible-doc","ansible-inventory","ansible-config"],0,"ansible-vault encrypts variable files or single strings with AES-256 and decrypts them at run time. The other tools display documentation, inventory or settings.","4.0 Automation: configuration management"],["lx59",0,4,"Before changing cloud infrastructure defined in OpenTofu, the team wants to see what would be created, changed or destroyed. Which command shows this without applying anything?",["tofu apply -auto-approve","tofu destroy","tofu plan","tofu fmt"],2,"plan compares the configuration with the state and prints the proposed changes. apply -auto-approve makes the changes without asking for confirmation.","4.0 Automation: infrastructure as code"],["lx60",0,4,"Which configuration management tool uses, by default, an agent on each node that pulls a compiled catalog from a central server?",["Puppet","Ansible","OpenTofu","Git"],0,"Puppet agents check in with the Puppet server and apply the catalog. Ansible is agentless and pushes changes over SSH.","4.0 Automation: configuration management"],["lx61",0,4,"Running ./backup.sh returns \"Permission denied\" even though the owner can read the file. What fixes this?",["chmod u+x backup.sh","chown root backup.sh","export PATH=backup.sh","source ~/.bashrc"],0,"The script needs execute permission to run as ./backup.sh. Running bash backup.sh would also work because bash only needs to read the file.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting"],["lx62",0,4,"What does this print: grep -q root /etc/passwd; echo $?",["0","1","2","root"],0,"grep -q prints nothing and exits 0 when a match is found. It would be 1 for no match and 2 for an error such as a missing file.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting"],["lx63",0,4,"APP_PORT is not set. What does echo \"${APP_PORT:-8080}\" print?",["APP_PORT","-8080","An empty line","8080"],3,"${var:-default} uses the default when the variable is unset or empty and does not assign it. ${var:=default} would also assign it.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting"],["lx64",0,4,"In a bash script, which test is true only when /opt/app exists and is a directory?",["[ -f /opt/app ]","[ -x /opt/app ]","[ -z /opt/app ]","[ -d /opt/app ]"],3,"-d tests for a directory. -f is true only for regular files, and -z tests whether a string is empty.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting"],["lx65",0,4,"What does this print: for i in {1..3}; do printf \"%s\" $i; done; echo",["1 2 3","{1..3}","123","3"],2,"Brace expansion produces 1 2 3, and printf \"%s\" adds no spaces or newlines, so the output is 123 followed by the newline from echo.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting"],["lx66",0,4,"A script runs curl ... | tee out.json and should stop if curl fails, but the pipeline always returns 0. Which option fixes this?",["set -x","set -u","set -o pipefail","set +e"],2,"By default a pipeline's exit status is the last command's (tee). pipefail returns the last non-zero status in the pipeline, so set -e can catch it. set -x only traces commands.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting"],["lx67",0,4,"What does this Python print: print(len({1, 2, 2, 3}))",["4","2","3","A TypeError"],2,"Curly braces with values create a set, which drops the duplicate 2, leaving three elements. A list [1, 2, 2, 3] would have length 4.","4.0 Automation: Python"],["lx68",0,4,"An AI assistant generated a shell script to delete old log files. What should the admin do before running it in production?",["Run it as root because AI output is pre-tested","Paste real credentials in to make it complete","Review it and test it on a non-production VM","Schedule it in cron before reading it through"],2,"Treat AI output like code from an unknown contributor: read it, test it somewhere safe and keep secrets out of prompts. A wrong path in a delete command can wipe data.","4.0 Automation: AI best practices"],["lx69",0,4,"A bad commit was already pushed to a shared main branch. Which command undoes it without rewriting history for other developers?",["git reset --hard HEAD~1, then force-push","git checkout -- .","git stash","git revert <commit>"],3,"git revert adds a new commit that reverses the bad one, so everyone else's history stays valid. reset plus force-push rewrites shared history and breaks other people's clones.","4.0 Automation: Git"],["lx70",0,4,"Which command creates a new branch named feature/login and switches to it?",["git branch -d feature/login","git merge feature/login","git clone feature/login","git switch -c feature/login"],3,"git switch -c (or git checkout -b) creates the branch and moves HEAD to it. git branch -d deletes a branch.","4.0 Automation: Git"],["lx71",0,5,"Writes to /srv fail with \"No space left on device\", but df -h shows /srv only 40% used. df -i shows IUse% at 100%. What is the cause?",["The disk was remounted read-only","The user's memory limit was reached","The filesystem has run out of inodes","The partition table is corrupted"],2,"Every file uses an inode, and millions of tiny files can use them all while blocks are still free. A read-only mount gives \"Read-only file system\", not \"No space left\".","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage"],["lx72",0,5,"df shows /var at 100%, but du -sh /var adds up to only 30% of the size. An old 20 GB log was deleted earlier. What is the most likely explanation?",["A running process still holds the deleted file open","du does not count files in hidden directories","The filesystem journal is using the space","The log was moved to swap by the kernel"],0,"Space is freed only when the last open handle closes. lsof +L1 shows deleted-but-open files; restart or signal the process to release the space. du does include hidden files.","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage"],["lx73",0,5,"uptime on a 2-CPU server shows load average: 8.10, 7.95, 7.80. What does this indicate?",["Far more tasks are runnable or waiting than CPUs","The CPUs are each about 8% busy on average","Eight users are logged in at the moment","The server has been up for about eight days"],0,"Load average counts tasks that are running, runnable or in uninterruptible (often I/O) wait. A sustained load of about 8 on 2 CPUs means heavy queuing. It is not a CPU percentage.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance"],["lx74",0,5,"A Java service keeps disappearing without writing an error to its own log. How can the admin confirm whether the kernel killed it for lack of memory?",["Check /etc/security/limits.conf for the service","Search the kernel log for OOM killer messages","Run free -h and look at the buff/cache column","Look for a core dump in the service's home"],1,"The OOM killer logs \"Out of memory: Killed process\" to the kernel ring buffer (journalctl -k or dmesg). free shows current memory use but not past kills.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance"],["lx75",0,5,"top shows low user CPU but a high wa (I/O wait) value. Which tool best shows which disk is saturated?",["iostat -x","lscpu","nproc","uname -r"],0,"iostat -x reports per-device utilization, queue size and await times (from the sysstat package). lscpu and nproc only describe CPU hardware.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance"],["lx76",0,5,"A server can ping 8.8.8.8 but ping example.com fails with \"Temporary failure in name resolution\". What should be checked first?",["DNS settings in /etc/resolv.conf, tested with dig","The default gateway in the routing table","Whether the NIC driver module is loaded","Whether the firewall blocks outbound ICMP"],0,"Reaching an internet IP proves the NIC, gateway and ICMP all work. The failure is name resolution, so check the configured resolvers and test them with dig or resolvectl.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking"],["lx77",0,5,"A new service fails to start with \"address already in use\" on port 8080. Which command shows which process already listens on that port?",["ip route show","ss -tulpn","ping -c 4 localhost","traceroute localhost"],1,"ss -tulpn lists TCP/UDP listening sockets with numeric ports and the owning process. ip route shows routing, not sockets.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking"],["lx78",0,5,"A host can reach other machines on 192.168.10.0/24 but nothing beyond. ip route shows only \"192.168.10.0/24 dev eth0 proto kernel scope link src 192.168.10.20\". What is missing?",["A default route through the gateway","An IPv6 link-local address","A DNS server in /etc/hosts","A second address on eth0"],0,"With only the connected route, the kernel has no path to other networks. Add default via 192.168.10.1 (persistently with nmcli or netplan). DNS would not stop traffic sent to raw IP addresses.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking"],["lx79",0,5,"systemctl status app shows \"Main process exited, code=exited, status=203/EXEC\". What is the most likely cause?",["The unit is masked by the administrator","Another process already holds its port","The ExecStart program is missing or not executable","The service ran out of memory at startup"],2,"Status 203/EXEC means systemd could not execute the command in ExecStart (wrong path, no execute bit or a bad shebang). A masked unit refuses to start and never reaches exec.","5.0 Troubleshooting: services"],["lx80",0,5,"After a typo in /etc/fstab, the server boots into emergency mode with the root filesystem read-only. What is the correct recovery?",["Reinstall GRUB2 to the boot disk first","Run mkfs on the root filesystem","Remount / read-write and fix /etc/fstab","Boot to graphical.target and retry"],2,"mount -o remount,rw / makes the root filesystem writable so fstab can be corrected; then run systemctl daemon-reload and reboot. The boot loader is fine because the kernel already started.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot"],["lx81",0,5,"A server boots to a graphical desktop, and the admin wants it to boot to a text console by default from now on. Which command does this?",["systemctl set-default multi-user.target","systemctl isolate multi-user.target","systemctl set-default rescue.target","systemctl enable graphical.target"],0,"set-default changes the default.target link used at boot. isolate switches targets now but does not change what happens at the next boot.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot"],["lx82",0,5,"The kernel logs ext4 errors for the data partition /dev/sdb1, mounted on /data. What is the safe way to check and repair it?",["Run fsck /dev/sdb1 while it stays mounted","Run xfs_repair /dev/sdb1 to fix it","Run mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1 to clear errors","Unmount it, then run fsck /dev/sdb1"],3,"fsck (e2fsck) must run on an unmounted filesystem, or it can cause more corruption. xfs_repair is for XFS, and mkfs would erase the data.","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage"],["lx83",0,5,"A web app on an SELinux-enforcing host is denied access to a file even after chmod 777. Where should the admin look to confirm the cause?",["ausearch -m avc for AVC denials","The output of ls -l on the file","The web server's access_log file","The /etc/sudoers.d directory"],0,"SELinux denials are logged as AVC records in the audit log; ausearch -m avc -ts recent shows them. Standard permissions are already wide open, so ls -l will not explain the denial.","5.0 Troubleshooting: security"],["lx84",0,5,"ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 fails with \"Permissions 0644 for '/home/ana/.ssh/id_ed25519' are too open\". What fixes it?",["chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub","chmod 777 ~/.ssh","chown root ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519"],3,"The SSH client refuses a private key other users can read. 600 limits it to the owner. The .pub file can stay readable because it is public.","5.0 Troubleshooting: security"],["lx85",0,5,"Kerberos and TLS logins on a server fail intermittently with clock-skew errors. Which command shows whether the host is synchronizing time and its current offset?",["hwclock --systohc","timedatectl set-timezone UTC","chronyc tracking","date +%s"],2,"chronyc tracking shows the reference source, stratum and system time offset. Changing the timezone does not fix skew, because UTC time is what matters to these protocols.","5.0 Troubleshooting: services"],["lx86",0,5,"Installing a .deb with dpkg -i fails because dependencies are missing, leaving the package half-configured. Which command resolves it?",["apt --fix-broken install","dpkg --purge --force-all","apt-mark hold","apt autoremove"],0,"apt --fix-broken install (apt -f install) downloads the missing dependencies and finishes configuring the package. Forced purges can leave the system inconsistent.","5.0 Troubleshooting: software"],["lx87",0,5,"Users report a slow server. Which command lists the processes using the most CPU, highest first?",["ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head","ps aux --sort=-%mem | head","free -m | head","lsof | head"],0,"--sort=-%cpu sorts by CPU usage in descending order. Sorting by %mem finds memory hogs instead, which does not match the symptom.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance"],["lx88",0,5,"dmesg shows repeated I/O errors and read retries on /dev/sda. Which command reports the drive's own health counters, such as reallocated sectors?",["fdisk -l /dev/sda","smartctl -a /dev/sda","blkid /dev/sda","hdparm -z /dev/sda"],1,"smartctl (smartmontools) reads SMART attributes and self-test results from the drive. fdisk -l shows only the partition layout.","5.0 Troubleshooting: hardware"],["lx89",0,5,"A branch office can reach its own gateway but not app01 at headquarters. Which tool shows the hop where packets stop?",["arp -a","hostname -I","traceroute app01","ethtool eth0"],2,"traceroute (or tracepath / mtr) lists each router hop until replies stop, which shows where the path breaks. arp -a only shows local Layer 2 neighbors.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking"],["lx90",0,5,"podman run -p 8080:80 web fails with \"address already in use\". What is the cause?",["The image does not expose port 80 in its metadata","Rootless containers cannot publish any ports","Another process already listens on host port 8080","Port 80 inside the container is already taken"],2,"The host side of the mapping is what conflicts; each container has its own network namespace, so port 80 inside is free. Rootless podman can publish ports at 1024 and above.","5.0 Troubleshooting: containers"],["lx91",0,5,"After a kernel update, a server fails to boot, but the previous kernel still works. What is the quickest way to get it running while investigating?",["Reinstall the OS from the installation media","Select the previous kernel from the GRUB menu","Run dracut -f from the firmware setup screen","Delete /boot and reboot with the old disk"],1,"GRUB keeps older kernels as menu entries. Boot the known-good one, then fix or remove the new kernel (and set the default with grubby or grub-set-default). Reinstalling is unnecessary.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot"]]);
